what REDS %790: rye/src/main.rye writes one memo record beside the rye binary per distinct subject it has ever keyed -- rye-key-cache.<digest>.kyri for a file, rye-key-library.<digest>.kyri for a library tree -- and nothing ever removes one. Measured 56 records, 5 live, 51 naming a deleted path. rye/bin/ is gitignored, so no meter in this tree can see the room and the only bound is the one the writer states. Build it: a named per-family ceiling on the record count, and an eviction after a write that deletes the oldest-written records past it, skipping the record just written and refusing any name that is not exactly prefix + 16 hex + .kyri so a peer's .writing temporary is never touched. Per family rather than across both, keeping the landing paper's single-strandedness -- a library build can never evict a file record. Deleting a record costs a rehash and never a wrong answer, which is the whole safety argument.
